## Suggested addition: explain the software cache in ordinary terms

This can follow the smaller-channel paragraph in section 26 or start the new integration section.

> A weight matrix is constant during inference, but its usual row/column layout is not the layout in which a warp's MMA instructions consume it. We prepare a second byte arrangement once so that each lane can fetch four adjacent 32-bit words with a 16-byte load. Those words were separate loads and address calculations in the canonical path. This changes where weights are stored, while preserving their FP8 bits and the order in which K contributes to every accumulator.
>
> The layout is `[group][K32][N16][lane][four words]`. A K32-by-N16 tile contains 512 FP8 bytes; 32 lanes × four words × four bytes also covers exactly 512 bytes. Groups are packed independently. This cache is a persistent software tensor, distinct from the GPU's hardware L2 cache. Preparation occurs before graph capture and outside resident timing.
>
> Each prepared tensor retains both layouts: slice 0 is canonical and slice 1 is the vector layout. The public fallback can therefore use the existing weight view without repacking. The tradeoff is one extra copy of the five eligible window-weight arrays.

| Width | One canonical copy | Total dual-layout storage | Additional storage |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| C64 | 45,056 bytes | 90,112 bytes | 45,056 bytes |
| C128 | 163,840 bytes | 327,680 bytes | 163,840 bytes |
| C256 | 622,592 bytes | 1,245,184 bytes | 622,592 bytes |

These are per-block totals for the eligible five arrays, not the entire model's parameter footprint. A full-model memory claim should use the prepared model's measured unique-storage count. Canonical tensor names are views of slice 0; they do not represent a third allocation. The `InferenceBlock._apply` hook restores those aliases after device moves or forced buffer copies, including direct moves of an individual block. The prepared snapshot is rebuilt after source-model weights change.

## Suggested new section: put measured cache choices behind C++ dispatch

The following is ready-to-adapt prose. Keep the historical v24 931-case native run distinct from the active v28 72-anchor policy. Its validation-status sentences must be refreshed after root finishes v28 testing and timing.

> The v27 experiments are promoted through a separate cached-window dispatcher. Python prepares immutable operands and registers the cache tensors; C++ validates the call and chooses the implementation. Forward execution does not repack weights or run a Python autotuner.
>
> The initial compiled table contains 72 SM120 anchors from 288 timing observations across 32 reports. These represent 144 distinct block/shape/storage/phase contracts, each measured in two orders. They collapse to 72 dispatch anchors because two real checkpoint blocks share each key. Policy version `86bfe65380016080` identifies these exact choices.
>
> A promoted candidate must take less than 97% of the fastest existing canonical/public/pair/composed time in every recorded block and timing-order observation for its key. We preserve every repeat rather than replacing a slower observation with a faster one. Among qualifying new candidates, the exporter prefers a common choice within 1% of each observation's fastest peer when possible. It does not require one new cache schedule to beat another nearly equivalent cache schedule by 3%.
>
> All 24 C256 anchors choose rolling B-prefetch. C128 uses cache variant 2 at 12 anchors and variant 3 at 12 anchors. C64 uses cache variant 0 once and variant 3 at 16 anchors; seven C64 anchors explicitly keep canonical selection because no common cache candidate meets the promotion rule. QKV and alternating-bank prefetch are not promoted by this table.
>
> The measured family is batch-1 FP8 with packed input and packed publication, covering all four normalized shift phases and both raw-side-output choices at three field sizes per width. The lookup matches architecture, channel width, precision, input storage, output storage, phase, batch and raw-output requirement. Within that family it selects the nearest measured shifted-window count, chooses the lower anchor on a tie, and uses the endpoint configuration outside the measured interval. Tensor dimensions and data remain unchanged.
>
> For example, C128 phase 1 without a raw side output has shifted-window anchors 286, 589 and 2,135. The first two use cache variant 2; the last uses variant 3. A count of 1,362 is exactly halfway between 589 and 2,135 and takes the lower anchor. A count of 1,363 takes the upper anchor. Counts below 286 and above 2,135 use the corresponding endpoint configuration. This is configuration transfer, not a new performance measurement at those sizes.
>
> Explicit fallback anchors participate in nearest selection too. They prevent a failed measured anchor from disappearing and unintentionally borrowing a faster-looking neighbor. Unknown architectures, unmeasured storage or batch families, Half weights, missing caches, and mixed canonical/cache operands use the existing canonical dispatcher. The fast route requires all five dual caches. FP16 inference and FP32/BF16 training are unchanged by this integration.
>
> The JSON table records the device, binary, source, measurement and validation provenance, and the generated C++ header is compiled into the extension. Native-component exactness and local sanitizer evidence support the transformation. They do not establish a whole-network or original-DLL speed win for the newly integrated binary.

A small table mapping public policy IDs would avoid confusion with private variant numbers:

| Public policy ID | Meaning |
|---|---|
| 0 | Existing canonical public dispatch |
| 1–4 | Cached variants 0–3 |
| 5 | Rolling B-prefetch |

There is no need to expose reserved, unpromoted IDs in the main reader-facing explanation.

### Continuous tuning is prepared, not yet measured in full

Suggested continuation:

> The cached-window range runner enumerates all 3,690,401 requested integer input pairs and their 931 actual padded geometries. Its CPU manifest contains 7,212 dispatch keys and 67,032 physical timing-order cases. It keeps actual block/shape memberships rather than combining unrelated blocks and shapes. This manifest is complete enumeration, not completed GPU tuning; the initial 72-anchor table is a smaller measured subset.
>
> Each GPU result is journaled. Resume identity includes GPU UUID, extension and source hashes, checkpoint, full requested membership, timing settings, driver, Torch/CUDA versions and compiled policy versions. A changed runtime or policy cannot silently reuse old timing records. Timestamps and free-memory readings are excluded because they are not stable experiment identity. Export requires the requested memberships to be complete; unfinished or failed cases remain visible.

Use `run_tuning.py window-cached-range --manifest-only` as the enumeration example, and `window-cached-generate` as the explicit completed-sweep promotion entry point. Do not imply these commands have completed a GPU sweep merely because the CPU scan succeeded.

### Measure integration on the same binary

Suggested continuation:

> Component improvements need a whole-network check. The paired benchmark captures two complete-model graphs using the same prepared model and cache weights: one forces the canonical fallback, and the other uses the public cached dispatcher. The benchmark temporarily redirects the operator for the eager canonical reference and baseline capture, restores it in `finally`, and captures both graphs before timing either. Normal and reversed timing rounds use equal invocation counts and retained-output budgets.
>
> The output head is FP32 with four channels. Retained bytes are computed from its actual shape and dtype; the budget applies per graph and the report also states the combined allocation. Every retained head is compared, poisoned and replayed three times, with input and weight mutation checks. This measures resident network work. It excludes preparation and renderer/NGX work, and it does not replace a fresh native comparison.

The first paired-model attempt found a benchmark bug before publishing timings: the expected head was copied from a graph output before that graph had replayed. CUDA capture records launches without executing them, so equal initial allocation bytes were not evidence of equal network results. The repaired benchmark keeps a separately executed eager canonical head, replays both graphs, then compares every captured head to that reference before poisoning or timing. Root's diagnostic confirmed this cause; three CPU regression cases now reject incorrect graph results even when their initial bytes match. Keep the failed report as a harness failure, not a model-parity result.

Until that v28 report exists, do not project the isolated 1.246–1.427× C256 gain onto the complete network. The older v20/v24 whole-model table is useful historical evidence, but it is not the same-binary cache experiment.

## Keep the unsuccessful experiments and their limits readable

Section 26's current explanations are technically sound. A compact comparison would make the lesson easier to retain without replacing the detailed SASS discussion:

| Experiment | Intended improvement | Measured or compiled limitation |
|---|---|---|
| Earlier pair-prefetch | Overlap two reduction groups | Uses the old scalar-weight load path; it did not clear the promotion gate. It is not equivalent to the later vector-cache rolling experiment. |
| QKV M32 | Reuse shared A across Q/K/V | Extra QKV B traffic and 175 registers; no win over rolling prefetch in the matched v27 cases. |
| QKV M64 | Share A without M32's duplicated B reads | More live accumulators and 208 registers; no win over rolling prefetch. |
| Alternating two-bank prefetch | Remove 504 carry moves per warp/window | The compiler delays next-bank loads in eight of nine loops; removal of moves does not preserve all intended overlap. It loses to rolling prefetch in all measured C256 observations. |
| Rolling B-prefetch | Start the next vector B loads before current MMAs | Wins the measured C256 resident cases, but reaches only 48.56% tensor throughput in the cited profile and still loses the native physical-layout speed comparison. |

Do not generalize these outcomes to every GPU or possible field. “Not selected by this measured policy” is more precise than “never useful.” The deeper-lookahead discussion is correctly labeled a hypothesis; preserve that wording until its own evidence is available.

## Preserve the exact timing and acceptance boundaries

The existing separation is good. Consider a compact reference table near section 9:

| Evidence | Included work | What it establishes |
|---|---|---|
| Resident component CUDA Graph | Prepared logical inputs/weights; operator outputs retained; cache preparation excluded | Relative component speed for the stated contract and binary |
| Native component comparison | Same native physical input/output layout; candidate gather/scatter included | Exact native published output and matched physical-boundary speed |
| Complete candidate model | Prepared feature input through FP32 head | Candidate network latency, not renderer or NGX latency |
| Native v24 trunk range | Resident blocks 1–69, internal transitions/pools, counter reset and candidate endpoint conversions | Exact stated trunk boundary at 931 geometries; speed gap at all 931 |
| Cold-cache NCU | Selected kernel under profiler replay | Resource-throughput/stall evidence under that profile's conditions |

Keep candidate/original ratio labels explicit: 1.910 means the candidate takes 1.910 times as long, not a 1.910× speedup. The 931-case median is unweighted across padded geometries and uses a fixed synthetic input recipe. It is not weighted by how many visible dimensions map to each geometry, and it is not a proof over every possible frame.

The operational 85% gate uses named NCU elapsed-cycle percent-of-peak-sustained metrics for L2, DRAM or tensor pipes. High hit rate, high instruction activity, summed percentages, or an isolated latency improvement do not pass it. The cited latest C256/C128/C64 tensor figures are 48.56% / 48.28% / 43.78%; all remain below 85%. A profiler throughput gate is not a full arithmetic-intensity roofline model, so avoid implying that such a model has been established if only these counters were collected.

The existing FP16 correction is important and should stay: literal PTX Half rounding was a false lead because the unchanged cubin contracts the relevant operations. C32's bounded native FP16 runtime proof and the global operand-order trace do not establish wider/global/full-model native FP16 runtime parity. CPU ABI/address proofs, if added later, must be labeled separately from GPU execution.

